  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":71,"t":"assess_parts","p":"For the arithmetic series 3 + 7 + 11 + …, find the first term and the common difference.\\n\\nb Find the sum of the first 15 terms, using Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × (a₁ + aₙ). Write down the last term clearly before you add.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the arithmetic series 3 + 7 + 11 + …, find the first term and the common difference."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Find the sum of the first 15 terms, using Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × (a₁ + aₙ). Write down the last term clearly before you add."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 3, d = 7 − 3 = 4. (b) u₁₅ = 3 + (15 − 1) × 4 = 3 + 56 = 59. S₁₅ = 15 ÷ 2 × (a₁ + a₁₅) = 15 ÷ 2 × (3 + 59) = 7.5 × 62 = 465.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for a₁ = 3 and d = 4 (both), 1 for d shown as a subtraction. (b) 3 marks: 1 for u₁₅ = 59 before summing (as instructed), 1 for the correct substitution into Sₙ = n/2 × (a₁ + aₙ), 1 for 465.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — u₁₅ = 59; S₁₅ = 465; ‘465’.","m":5,"df":"easy","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":71,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Write the 15th term first, then average and multiply.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) first term and d","prompt":"Find the first term and the common difference.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Read the first term from the series.","Subtract consecutive terms to get the common difference."],"accepted":["a₁ = 3, d = 4","first term 3, common difference 4","3 and 4","a=3, d=4"],"keywords":[["3"],["4"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) the 15th term","prompt":"Write down the last term clearly before you add.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Use the nth term formula with your own a₁ and d at n = 15.","Evaluate the nth term formula at n = 15."],"answer":59,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) sum of the first 15 terms","prompt":"Find the sum of the first 15 terms, using Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × (a₁ + aₙ).","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use n ÷ 2 × (a₁ + aₙ) with your own end terms.","Substitute the end terms into n ÷ 2 × (a₁ + aₙ)."],"answer":465,"tolerance":0}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 3, d = 7 − 3 = 4. (b) u₁₅ = 3 + (15 − 1) × 4 = 3 + 56 = 59. S₁₅ = 15 ÷ 2 × (a₁ + a₁₅) = 15 ÷ 2 × (3 + 59) = 7.5 × 62 = 465.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for a₁ = 3 and d = 4 (both), 1 for d shown as a subtraction. (b) 3 marks: 1 for u₁₅ = 59 before summing (as instructed), 1 for the correct substitution into Sₙ = n/2 × (a₁ + aₙ), 1 for 465.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — u₁₅ = 59; S₁₅ = 465; ‘465’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 3, d = 7 − 3 = 4. (b) u₁₅ = 3 + (15 − 1) × 4 = 3 + 56 = 59. S₁₅ = 15 ÷ 2 × (a₁ + a₁₅) = 15 ÷ 2 × (3 + 59) = 7.5 × 62 = 465.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for a₁ = 3 and d = 4 (both), 1 for d shown as a subtraction. (b) 3 marks: 1 for u₁₅ = 59 before summing (as instructed), 1 for the correct substitution into Sₙ = n/2 × (a₁ + aₙ), 1 for 465.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — u₁₅ = 59; S₁₅ = 465; ‘465’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 3, d = 7 − 3 = 4. (b) u₁₅ = 3 + (15 − 1) × 4 = 3 + 56 = 59. S₁₅ = 15 ÷ 2 × (a₁ + a₁₅) = 15 ÷ 2 × (3 + 59) = 7.5 × 62 = 465.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for a₁ = 3 and d = 4 (both), 1 for d shown as a subtraction. (b) 3 marks: 1 for u₁₅ = 59 before summing (as instructed), 1 for the correct substitution into Sₙ = n/2 × (a₁ + aₙ), 1 for 465.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — u₁₅ = 59; S₁₅ = 465; ‘465’."},{"n":72,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Find the number of terms in the arithmetic series 13 + 17 + 21 + … + 133.\\n\\nb Hence find the sum of the series.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Find the number of terms in the arithmetic series 13 + 17 + 21 + … + 133."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Hence find the sum of the series."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 13, d = 4, last term 133: 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133 → (n − 1) × 4 = 120 → n − 1 = 30 → n = 31 terms. (b) S₃₁ = 31 ÷ 2 × (13 + 133) = 15.5 × 146 = 2263.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the equation 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133, 1 for n = 31. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the correct sum formula substitution (n = 31, 13, 133), 1 for 15.5 × 146 evaluated coherently, 1 for 2263.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘31 terms’; ‘2263’.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":72,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Count the terms with the nth-term formula, then sum.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) number of terms","prompt":"Find the number of terms in the series.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133.","Solve the equation; the solution is the number of terms."],"answer":31,"tolerance":0,"unit":"terms"},{"id":"b","label":"(b) the sum of the series","prompt":"Hence find the sum of the series.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use your own term count in n ÷ 2 × (first + last).","Evaluate the sum formula with your count and the end terms."],"answer":2263,"tolerance":0}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 13, d = 4, last term 133: 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133 → (n − 1) × 4 = 120 → n − 1 = 30 → n = 31 terms. (b) S₃₁ = 31 ÷ 2 × (13 + 133) = 15.5 × 146 = 2263.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the equation 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133, 1 for n = 31. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the correct sum formula substitution (n = 31, 13, 133), 1 for 15.5 × 146 evaluated coherently, 1 for 2263.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘31 terms’; ‘2263’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 13, d = 4, last term 133: 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133 → (n − 1) × 4 = 120 → n − 1 = 30 → n = 31 terms. (b) S₃₁ = 31 ÷ 2 × (13 + 133) = 15.5 × 146 = 2263.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the equation 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133, 1 for n = 31. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the correct sum formula substitution (n = 31, 13, 133), 1 for 15.5 × 146 evaluated coherently, 1 for 2263.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘31 terms’; ‘2263’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = 13, d = 4, last term 133: 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133 → (n − 1) × 4 = 120 → n − 1 = 30 → n = 31 terms. (b) S₃₁ = 31 ÷ 2 × (13 + 133) = 15.5 × 146 = 2263.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the equation 13 + (n − 1) × 4 = 133, 1 for n = 31. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the correct sum formula substitution (n = 31, 13, 133), 1 for 15.5 × 146 evaluated coherently, 1 for 2263.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘31 terms’; ‘2263’."},{"n":73,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A carpenter makes 12 chairs in the first week. Each week he makes 3 more chairs than the week before. Find the number of chairs he makes in the 10th week.\\n\\nb Find the total number of chairs he makes in the first 10 weeks, and state whether he reaches his target of 250 chairs. Justify your answer with a calculation.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A carpenter makes 12 chairs in the first week. Each week he makes 3 more chairs than the week before. Find the number of chairs he makes in the 10th week."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Find the total number of chairs he makes in the first 10 weeks, and state whether he reaches his target of 250 chairs. Justify your answer with a calculation."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) u₁₀ = 12 + (10 − 1) × 3 = 12 + 27 = 39 chairs. (b) S₁₀ = 10 ÷ 2 × (12 + 39) = 5 × 51 = 255 chairs. Target check: 255 ≥ 250 — yes, he reaches his target in the first 10 weeks, with 5 chairs to spare.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the method (12 + 9 × 3), 1 for 39. (b) 3 marks: 2 for S₁₀ = 255 (1 substitution, 1 value), 1 for the justified conclusion (255 ≥ 250, ‘yes’, with the comparison shown).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘255 chairs’, ‘yes, exceeds by 5’.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":73,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"10th week, then the sum, then compare with 250.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) chairs in the 10th week","prompt":"Find the number of chairs he makes in the 10th week.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["u₁₀ = 12 + (10 − 1) × 3.","Evaluate the nth term formula at n = 10."],"answer":39,"tolerance":0,"unit":"chairs"},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) total in the first 10 weeks","prompt":"Find the total number of chairs he makes in the first 10 weeks.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use the sum formula with your own tenth term.","Substitute the first and tenth terms into the sum formula."],"answer":255,"tolerance":0,"unit":"chairs"},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) does he reach the target?","prompt":"State whether he reaches his target of 250 chairs. Justify with a calculation.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Compare your total with 250 and state the difference.","Say whether the total clears the target and by how much."],"accepted":["yes — 255 chairs in total, which is 5 more than the 250 target","he reaches it: 255 ≥ 250 (5 chairs above target)"],"keywords":[["yes"],["255"],["250","target"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) u₁₀ = 12 + (10 − 1) × 3 = 12 + 27 = 39 chairs. (b) S₁₀ = 10 ÷ 2 × (12 + 39) = 5 × 51 = 255 chairs. Target check: 255 ≥ 250 — yes, he reaches his target in the first 10 weeks, with 5 chairs to spare.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the method (12 + 9 × 3), 1 for 39. (b) 3 marks: 2 for S₁₀ = 255 (1 substitution, 1 value), 1 for the justified conclusion (255 ≥ 250, ‘yes’, with the comparison shown).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘255 chairs’, ‘yes, exceeds by 5’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) u₁₀ = 12 + (10 − 1) × 3 = 12 + 27 = 39 chairs. (b) S₁₀ = 10 ÷ 2 × (12 + 39) = 5 × 51 = 255 chairs. Target check: 255 ≥ 250 — yes, he reaches his target in the first 10 weeks, with 5 chairs to spare.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the method (12 + 9 × 3), 1 for 39. (b) 3 marks: 2 for S₁₀ = 255 (1 substitution, 1 value), 1 for the justified conclusion (255 ≥ 250, ‘yes’, with the comparison shown).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘255 chairs’, ‘yes, exceeds by 5’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) u₁₀ = 12 + (10 − 1) × 3 = 12 + 27 = 39 chairs. (b) S₁₀ = 10 ÷ 2 × (12 + 39) = 5 × 51 = 255 chairs. Target check: 255 ≥ 250 — yes, he reaches his target in the first 10 weeks, with 5 chairs to spare.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the method (12 + 9 × 3), 1 for 39. (b) 3 marks: 2 for S₁₀ = 255 (1 substitution, 1 value), 1 for the justified conclusion (255 ≥ 250, ‘yes’, with the comparison shown).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘255 chairs’, ‘yes, exceeds by 5’."},{"n":74,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series is Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × (3n + 7). Find the first term and the common difference.\\n\\nb Find the 20th term of the series, and explain how you used the formula for Sₙ to do it.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series is Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × (3n + 7). Find the first term and the common difference."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Find the 20th term of the series, and explain how you used the formula for Sₙ to do it."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = S₁ = (1 ÷ 2) × (3 + 7) = 5. The nth term: uₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁ = [n(3n + 7) − (n − 1)(3(n−1) + 7)] ÷ 2 = [3n² + 7n − (3n² + 4n − 3n − 4)] ÷ 2 = (6n + 4) ÷ 2 = 3n + 2. The terms are 5, 8, 11, … so d = 3. (b) Using Sₙ: S₂₀ = 20 ÷ 2 × (67) = 670; S₁₉ = 19 ÷ 2 × (64) = 608; u₂₀ = 670 − 608 = 62. (Directly: uₙ = 3n + 2 gives u₂₀ = 62 ✓ — both methods agree.)\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for a₁ = S₁ = 5, 1 for setting up uₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁, 1 for d = 3 (any correct derivation). (b) 2 marks: 1 for u₂₀ = 62, 1 for explaining the Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁ method with the two partial sums shown.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — d = 3 from the linear nth-term rule; ‘u₂₀ = 62’ by either method; explanation mentions partial-sum differences.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":74,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"a₁ = S₁; and uₙ always hides in Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) first term","prompt":"Find the first term.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The first term is S₁ — the sum of one term.","Evaluate the given formula at n = 1."],"answer":5,"tolerance":0},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) common difference","prompt":"Find the common difference.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The difference of consecutive partial sums gives uₙ — write uₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁.","Expand and simplify; the common difference is the coefficient of n."],"answer":3,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) the 20th term","prompt":"Find the 20th term of the series.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["u₂₀ = S₂₀ − S₁₉ — or evaluate your own nth term at 20.","Use your nth term at 20, or subtract consecutive partial sums."],"answer":62,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) how Sₙ was used","prompt":"Explain how you used the formula for Sₙ to do it.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["The nth term is the difference between consecutive partial sums: uₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁.","Evaluate S₂₀ and S₁₉ from the given formula, then subtract."],"accepted":["uₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁; u₂₀ = S₂₀ − S₁₉ = 670 − 608 = 62","the 20th term is the difference of the sums of the first 20 and first 19 terms"],"keywords":[["Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁","S(n) - S(n-1)","difference","consecutive","Sn - Sn-1"],["670"],["608"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) a₁ = S₁ = (1 ÷ 